ral arrow-shaped objects, each in a distinct non-black color. Each arrow consists of a straight body segment (horizontal or vertical, at least 2 pixels long and at most 5) plus a one-pixel arrowhead that branches from one end of the body in a perpendicular direction, indicating the arrow's pointing direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:164:  - For a horizontal body, the arrowhead is one pixel above or below the last pixel, indicating up or down.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:165:  - For a vertical body, the arrowhead is one pixel left or right of the last pixel, indicating left or right.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:166:- Arrows do not overlap. There are at least 2 and at most 5 arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:169:- For each arrow, determine its pointing direction (up, down, left, or right) from the arrowhead position relative to the body.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:170:- Let L = the body length (number of pixels in the body, not counting the arrowhead).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:171:- Starting from the arrowhead pixel, trace a path of L pixels in the pointing direction. If the path reaches a grid boundary before L steps, it reflects (bounces): the component of direction perpendicular to the hit boundary reverses (like a billiard ball), and tracing continues for the remaining steps.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:172:- Draw all L traced pixels in the arrow's color. The original arrow (body + arrowhead) remains unchanged.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:173:- If a traced pixel lands on a cell already occupied by another color, the later-drawn color overwrites. (Processing order: arrows are processed from top-to-bottom, left-to-right by their arrowhead position.)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:176:The arrow encodes both direction and distance: the body length is the trail length, and the arrowhead orientation sets the initial travel direction. The billiard-bounce reflection off grid edges means trails can change direction mid-flight, producing paths that are not simply straight lines -- solvers must simulate the reflection to predict where each trail goes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/10/des.md:194:- Since each shape matches exactly one edge, and top/bottom narrows to a row (2 cells) while left/right narrows to a column (2 cells), the intersection uniquely assigns each shape to one cell: the top-left cell gets the color shared by top-edge and left-edge matches (but since each shape matches only one edge, the cell is determined by the pair of shapes whose edges intersect at that cell -- specifically, each cell is at the intersection of one horizontal-edge color and one vertical-edge color, and receives the shape whose color matches the horizontal edge).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed247695927/4/prompt.md:334:Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed44865127/11/prompt.md:86:Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed44865127/11/prompt.md:223:Trace diagonal rays from triangular arrows, reflecting off colored wall pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed44865127/11/prompt.md:226:- bg grid with monochrome 3-pixel L-shaped \"arrows\" (2x2 bbox missing one corner) and scattered colored wall pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed44865127/11/prompt.md:229:- Each arrow fires a ray diagonally away from its missing corner, painting bg cells in its color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed44865127/11/prompt.md:232:- Ray stops when it exits the grid or reaches another arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/4/prompt.md:261:Trace diagonal rays from triangular arrows, reflecting off colored wall pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1354760211/4/prompt.md:264:- bg grid with